---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../components/DocsHandoff.astro';

const pageTitle = 'How Registry Stack fits | Registry Stack';
const pageDescription =
  'Registry Stack is deployed next to the source registry and before any exchange layer. See how it relates to identity systems, catalogs, and evidence exchange.';

const relationships = [
  {
    system: 'Existing registry software',
    examples: 'OpenCRVS, OpenSPP, DHIS2, and custom systems',
    label: 'Works alongside',
    body:
      'The existing system keeps its records, write workflows, and user interface. Registry Relay can serve reviewed SQLite views prepared from it, while Evidence Gateway can use a predefined HTTP source contract. The technical docs include reviewed OpenCRVS- and DHIS2-shaped examples, not automatic integrations with every deployment.',
  },
  {
    system: 'Files and databases',
    examples: 'Reviewed SQLite views and institution-owned extracts',
    label: 'Uses a reviewed handoff',
    body:
      'Registry Relay reads reviewed, read-only SQLite views. A CSV, XLSX, Parquet file, PostgreSQL database, or another source must first be converted to a reviewed SQLite view or extract by a process the institution owns and Relay does not see.',
  },
  {
    system: 'Exchange and integration layers',
    examples: 'X-Road, WSO2, and OpenFn',
    label: 'Connects after Registry Stack',
    body:
      'Registry Stack is deployed next to the source registry, before any exchange layer. It applies the registry owner’s rules first. An exchange layer can then transport requests and responses between institutions. Registry Stack does not replace that layer, and no X-Road, WSO2, or OpenFn integration is shipped today.',
  },
  {
    system: 'Identity and access systems',
    examples: 'Institutional OIDC providers and Registry Mint',
    label: 'Relies on verified identity',
    body:
      'Registry Stack verifies service access tokens from a configured issuer. It does not enroll people, issue national identifiers, or replace identity and access management. Registry Mint can issue short-lived tokens for machine clients when a deployment has no suitable authorization server.',
  },
  {
    system: 'Signed assertions and wallet delivery',
    examples: 'Signed JWS, SD-JWT VC, and an optional OID4VCI adapter',
    label: 'Supports bounded profiles',
    body:
      'Evidence Gateway returns signed JWS by default and can encode the same assertion as SD-JWT VC. An optional adapter delivers holder-bound Evidence credentials through a bounded OID4VCI 1.0 Final profile. The adapter adds no signing or Evidence semantics, and the stack is not a general wallet or credential-lifecycle platform.',
  },
  {
    system: 'Catalogs and standards',
    examples: 'DCAT, BRegDCAT-AP, CPSV-AP, JSON Schema, and OpenAPI',
    label: 'Publishes supported formats',
    body:
      'Registry Manifest publishes portable metadata such as DCAT-family and CPSV-AP descriptions. Relay generates deployment-specific OpenAPI, JSON Schema, SHACL, contexts, and discovery metadata from its compiled contract. Support and evidence differ by standard, so Registry Stack makes no blanket conformance claim.',
  },
  {
    system: 'Once-only evidence exchange',
    examples: 'Discover evidence, request it from an authority, return it to a service',
    label: 'Follows the same broad evidence journey',
    body:
      'Registry Stack describes available evidence and handles defined requests between authorities and services. An EU OOTS deployment would add its network, eDelivery, AS4 transport, and conformance requirements through the surrounding integration layer.',
  },
];
---

<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <SiteHeader current="how-it-fits" />

  <main>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="fit-hero-title">
      <div class="hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">How it fits</p>
          <h1 id="fit-hero-title">Registry Stack works with the systems a government already operates.</h1>
          <p class="hero-lead">
            Registry Stack is not a replacement for registry software, exchange networks,
            identity systems, or catalogs. It runs next to the registry and applies the registry
            owner&rsquo;s rules when another service requests an answer or selected data.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="relationships-title">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Existing systems and standards</p>
          <h2 id="relationships-title">What Registry Stack adds to each part of the environment.</h2>
          <p>
            Some connections are implemented and demonstrated today. Others describe where
            Registry Stack can sit but still require integration work. Each entry states the
            current level of support.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="relationship-list">
          {relationships.map((item) => (
            <article class="relationship-row">
              <div>
                <p class="relationship-label">{item.label}</p>
                <h3>{item.system}</h3>
                <p class="relationship-examples">{item.examples}</p>
              </div>
              <p class="relationship-body">{item.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="ownership-title">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="section-heading center">
          <p class="eyebrow">A direct service request</p>
          <h2 id="ownership-title">Registry Stack sits between the source registry and any exchange layer.</h2>
          <p>
            The components below are ordered from the source registry outward. A request travels
            inward in the opposite direction, and the response returns through the same
            components.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ol class="ownership-flow">
          <li>
            <span class="flow-number">01</span>
            <h3>The existing registry</h3>
            <p>Stores the source records. The registry owner remains responsible for updates and corrections.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="flow-number">02</span>
            <h3>Registry Stack</h3>
            <p>Runs next to the registry, checks each request, reads the source, returns the agreed answer or fields, and records the event.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="flow-number">03</span>
            <h3>The exchange layer, if used</h3>
            <p>Transports requests and responses between institutions after Registry Stack has applied the registry owner&rsquo;s rules.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="flow-number">04</span>
            <h3>The public or partner service</h3>
            <p>Sends the permitted request and uses the answer or selected fields it receives.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="product-path-note">
          Evidence Gateway returns signed facts. Registry Relay returns governed, selected data.
          Evidence Gateway may use a Relay-protected exact lookup as one predefined HTTP source,
          but each product keeps its own authorization, configuration, and audit boundary.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <DocsHandoff
    eyebrow="Technical details"
    heading="Review the supported integrations and standards before planning a deployment."
    primaryLabel="Read the docs"
    emphasis="docs"
  />
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .relationship-list {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--registry-rule);
  }

  .relationship-row {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--registry-rule);
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(20px, 4vw, 56px);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(240px, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.4fr);
    padding: 24px 0;
  }

  .relationship-row h3,
  .relationship-row p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .relationship-label {
    color: var(--registry-stamp);
    font-family: var(--registry-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .relationship-examples {
    color: var(--registry-blue);
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .relationship-body {
    color: var(--registry-body);
  }

  .ownership-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .ownership-flow li {
    background: #ffffff;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--registry-ink);
    padding: 18px;
    position: relative;
  }

  .ownership-flow h3 {
    margin: 12px 0 8px;
  }

  .ownership-flow p {
    color: var(--registry-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .flow-number {
    color: var(--registry-stamp);
    font-family: var(--registry-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .product-path-note {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--registry-blue);
    color: var(--registry-body);
    margin: 28px 0 0;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 18px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 901px) {
    .ownership-flow li:not(:last-child)::after {
      color: var(--registry-blue);
      content: '\2192';
      font-size: 22px;
      font-weight: 700;
      position: absolute;
      right: -17px;
      top: 28px;
      z-index: 1;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .ownership-flow {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .relationship-row,
    .ownership-flow {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
